“Real-time SonarQube” has two parts:

1. While coding: install SonarQube for IDE in IntelliJ for Spring Boot and VS Code/WebStorm for React, then bind both folders to their SonarQube projects. It flags supported issues as you type and synchronizes server rules, exclusions, and issue status. [Official connected-mode guide](https://docs.sonarsource.com/sonarqube-server/2025.4/user-guide/connected-mode)
2. Authoritative CI checks: run unit tests + coverage + scanner on every push/PR. SonarQube computes the Quality Gate after analysis. [How analysis works](https://docs.sonarsource.com/sonarqube-server/2025.4/analyzing-source-code/analysis-overview)

A practical repository layout:

text
my-product/
├── backend/       # Spring Boot / Maven
├── frontend/      # React / Vite
└── .github/workflows/sonarqube.yml

## 1. Run SonarQube locally

`docker-compose.yml`:

yaml
services:
  sonarqube:
    image: sonarqube:lts-community
    container_name: sonarqube
    ports:
      - "9000:9000"
    environment:
      SONAR_ES_BOOTSTRAP_CHECKS_DISABLE: "true"
    volumes:
      - sonarqube_data:/opt/sonarqube/data
      - sonarqube_extensions:/opt/sonarqube/extensions
      - sonarqube_logs:/opt/sonarqube/logs

volumes:
  sonarqube_data:
  sonarqube_extensions:
  sonarqube_logs:

Start it:

bash
docker compose up -d

Open `http://localhost:9000`, sign in initially with `admin` / `admin`, change that password, then create:

- Project: `acme-shop-api`, key: `acme-shop-api`
- Project: `acme-shop-web`, key: `acme-shop-web`
- A scoped analysis token for CI/local analysis

For production, use a supported SonarQube Server edition with PostgreSQL and persistent storage—not this lightweight local setup.

