## 4. GitHub Actions: enforce the Quality Gate

`.github/workflows/sonarqube.yml`:

yaml
name: Build, Test, and SonarQube

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  backend:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    defaults:
      run:
        working-directory: backend

    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v6
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0

      - uses: actions/setup-java@v5
        with:
          distribution: temurin
          java-version: "21"

      - name: Build, test, coverage, and scan backend
        run: >
          ./mvnw clean verify sonar:sonar
          -Dsonar.host.url=${{ secrets.SONAR_HOST_URL }}
          -Dsonar.token=${{ secrets.SONAR_TOKEN }}
          -Dsonar.qualitygate.wait=true

  frontend:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    defaults:
      run:
        working-directory: frontend

    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v6
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0

      - uses: actions/setup-node@v5
        with:
          node-version: "20"
          cache: npm
          cache-dependency-path: frontend/package-lock.json

      - name: Install and test with coverage
        run: |
          npm ci
          npm run test:coverage
          npm run build

      - name: Scan frontend
        run: >
          npx sonar
          --sonar.host.url=${{ secrets.SONAR_HOST_URL }}
          --sonar.token=${{ secrets.SONAR_TOKEN }}
          --sonar.qualitygate.wait=true

Set repository secrets:

text
SONAR_HOST_URL=https://sonarqube.company.internal
SONAR_TOKEN=<project-analysis-token>

Use a token in secrets, never in `pom.xml`, `package.json`, or committed properties files.

## 5. Recommended Quality Gate policy

Start with “new code” rules so legacy code does not block every delivery:

| Metric on new code | Practical starting threshold |
|---|---:|
| New bugs | 0 |
| New vulnerabilities | 0 |
| New security hotspots reviewed | 100% |
| New code coverage | ≥ 80% |
| New duplicated lines | ≤ 3% |
| Quality Gate | Must pass |

This makes the gate a delivery control, not merely a dashboard.

## Key FAQs

Is SonarQube truly real time?  
Not as a server scan after every keystroke. Use SonarQube for IDE for immediate editor feedback; use CI analysis as the shared, enforceable record.

Does SonarQube replace tests, ESLint, Checkstyle, Snyk, or code review?  
No. It complements them. Keep unit/integration tests; use ESLint for fast frontend conventions; use dependency/security tools as needed; retain human review.

Why is coverage 0%?  
Usually tests did not run before the scan, the report path is wrong, or the scanner cannot see the generated report. Verify:

bash
backend/target/site/jacoco/jacoco.xml
frontend/coverage/lcov.info

Why is React code not analyzed?  
Check `sonar.sources=src`, ensure `src` exists from the scanner’s working directory, and do not accidentally exclude it with `sonar.exclusions`.

Why is Java analysis incomplete?  
Build first. Java analysis needs compiled bytecode, so use `mvn clean verify sonar:sonar`, not only `sonar:sonar`.

Can one SonarQube project scan backend and frontend?  
Yes, but two projects are usually clearer: separate ownership, coverage, quality history, and release lifecycles. Use a single project only when both are always built and released together.

Can Community Edition analyze branches and pull requests?  
Edition capabilities differ. Confirm your chosen SonarQube edition before designing PR decoration and branch strategy. PR analysis is designed to analyze issues introduced by the PR and use the project Quality Gate. [PR analysis overview](https://docs.sonarsource.com/sonarqube-server/2025.4/analyzing-source-code/pull-request-analysis/introduction)

Why does the scanner fail in CI but work locally?  
Typical causes: unreachable server URL, untrusted TLS certificate, proxy/firewall, missing token, shallow Git checkout, unsupported Node/Java runtime, or missing coverage files. For current JS/TS scanning, Node must meet the scanner’s stated minimum; current documentation lists Node 18.20.0+. [NPM scanner requirements](https://docs.sonarsource.com/sonarqube-server/2025.4/analyzing-source-code/scanners/npm/installing)

Should I set `sonar.qualitygate.wait=true`?  
Yes for a blocking CI gate. It causes the scanner job to wait for server-side processing and fail the workflow when the gate fails.

Should I suppress findings?  
Only after reviewing them. Prefer fixing the code; otherwise mark a finding as False Positive or Accepted/Won’t Fix with a recorded rationale. Avoid broad exclusions and blanket `NOSONAR`.

Should tests be analyzed as production source?  
No. Define test paths correctly. Tests contribute coverage and test reporting, while main source is judged for production quality.

How do I avoid analyzing generated code?  
Exclude only generated/vendor/build directories, for example:

properties
sonar.exclusions=/generated/,/node_modules/,/dist/,/coverage/

Does SonarQube find dependency vulnerabilities?  
That depends on your SonarQube edition and enabled capabilities. Dependency analysis has specific edition and package-manager requirements; keep `pom.xml`, `package.json`, and lockfiles available to the scanner. [Dependency analysis guide](https://docs.sonarsource.com/sonarqube-server/2025.4/advanced-security/analyzing-projects-for-dependencies)

The most useful next step is to apply this to your actual repository structure—Maven vs Gradle, React vs Next.js, and GitHub/GitLab/Jenkins—so paths, commands, and CI configuration match exactly.

`-Dsonar.token=YOUR_TOKEN` is your secret authentication token that lets Maven/CI upload analysis results to SonarQube.

Create it in SonarQube:

1. Open `http://localhost:9000`
2. Click your profile icon → My Account
3. Open Security
4. Generate a token, for example `local-api-scan`
5. Copy it immediately—it is shown only once.

Then run:

bash
mvn clean verify sonar:sonar \
  -Dsonar.host.url=http://localhost:9000 \
  -Dsonar.token=paste_your_real_token_here

Example:

bash
-Dsonar.token=sqp_1234567890abcdef

Do not commit the token into `pom.xml` or Git.

`<sonar.projectKey>acme-shop-api</sonar.projectKey>` is different: it is the permanent ID of the SonarQube project that receives your backend results.

In your `pom.xml`:

xml
<properties>
  <sonar.projectKey>acme-shop-api</sonar.projectKey>
  <sonar.projectName>Acme Shop API</sonar.projectName>
</properties>

It must exactly match the Project Key created in the SonarQube web dashboard.

So:

- `sonar.projectKey`: public, stable project identifier
- `sonar.projectName`: display name in SonarQube
- `sonar.token`: private password-like credential used by scanners/CI